⏺ ADS Architecture Summary

  I've created a knowledge base for you. Here's the quick overview of the four systems:

  1. User Authentication [auth_architecture.md (link)]

  ADS uses a two-tier system:
  - Classic ADS backend — external legacy system validates credentials via HTTP
  - Local MongoDB — tracks users in ads_users collection with fields like cookie_id, username, developer_key, active, anonymous

  Anonymous users get a UUID cookie; real users have anonymous=False and sync metadata from classic ADS on login.

  2. API Permissions [api_permissions.md (link)]

  No request-rate-limiting — instead, capability-based access control with 3 tiers:
  - Basic: 100 rows max, 10k pagination limit, no facets/highlight
  - Devel: 200 rows, 50k pagination, facets (limit 100), highlight enabled
  - Collab: 200 rows, unlimited pagination, extended fields, facets (limit 500)

  Enforced via:
  - Developer key (dev_key query param) → hashed with SHA512 salt
  - IP whitelisting (optional CIDR ranges per user)
  - Permission check on each API call validates fields, rows, pagination, facet/highlight settings
  - All endpoints require @api_user_required + @api_ip_allowed decorators

  3. Saved Searches & Libraries [libraries_searches.md (link)]

  Managed via mongogut library interface in /adsgut module:
  - MongoDB-backed, user-scoped collections
  - HTTP endpoints for CRUD with filtering/sorting/pagination
  - Accessible via criteria, query, sort, pagtuple URL parameters
  - Custom JSON encoder handles ObjectId/DateTime serialization
  - No API rate limiting — only web interface rate limits apply

  4. Anonymous vs Authenticated Differences

  - Anonymous: UUID cookie, anonymous=True, limited to basic API permissions, no saved data
  - Authenticated: Real email/password from classic ADS, anonymous=False, can have dev keys, all permissions available, libraries/searches
  persisted

